4, "like_count": 0, "quote_count": 0, "reply_count": 1, "retweet_count": 0}, "referenced_tweets": [{"id": "2118449808402681911", "type": "replied_to"}], "text": "@dwarkesh_sp @grok whos doing this with chess"}, {"author_id": "1209960539390201864", "conversation_id": "2118449808402681911", "created_at": "2026-11-05T21:06:07.000Z", "edit_history_tweet_ids": ["2118449808402681911"], "entities": {"urls": [{"display_url": "pic.x.com/35ym1NcVDL", "end": 296, "expanded_url": "https://x.com/dwarkesh_sp/status/2118449808402681911/video/1", "media_key": "13_2118447221465100289", "start": 273, "url": "https://t.co/35ym1NcVDL"}]}, "id": "2118449808402681911", "note_tweet": {"entities": {"urls": [{"display_url": "clocktowerbench.com", "end": 811, "expanded_url": "http://clocktowerbench.com", "start": 788, "url": "https://t.co/IF4d0EMSD8"}]}, "text": "I spent 20,000 dollars in API credits having different AI models play each other at Blood on the Clocktower (a Mafia or Werewolf type social deception game).\n\nThe results were fascinating. Models have gotten much better over the course of this year at creative on-the-spot scheming and bluffing, and also at reasoning as a team to break apart these underground conspiracies.\n\nI go through some of the most standout games these AIs played in this video. The new models are getting pretty good.  If my friends pulled off some of these moves while we were playing, I’d give them a fist bump.\n\nFwiw, OpenAI’s newer models are much better than Anthropic’s at this game.\n\nYou can see the full benchmark at the website, and I’ve also made it so that you and your friends can play again the AIs: https://t.co/IF4d0EMSD8"}, "public_metrics": {"bookmark_count": 485, "impression_count": 89459, "like_count": 943, "quote_count": 33, "reply_count": 74, "retweet_count": 41}, "text": "I spent 20,000 dollars in API credits having different AI models play each other at Blood on the Clocktower (a Mafia or Werewolf type social deception game).\n\nThe results were fascinating. Models have gotten much better over the course of this year at creative on-the-spot https://t.co/35ym1NcVDL"}, {"author_id": "951180501896843264", "conversation_id": "2118449808402681911", "created_at": "2026-11-05T21:12:08.000Z", "edit_history_tweet_ids": ["2118451319061332429"], "entities": {"mentions": [{"end": 12, "id": "1209960539390201864", "start": 0, "username": "dwarkesh_sp"}]}, "id": "2118451319061332429", "public_metrics": {"bookmark_count": 0, "impression_count": 426, "like_count": 7, "quote_count": 0, "reply_count": 1, "retweet_count": 0}, "referenced_tweets": [{"id": "2118449808402681911", "type": "replied_to"}], "text": "@dwarkesh_sp Rare bench where training on alignment data probably harmful"}, {"author_id": "1728813295119794176", "conversation_id": "2118449808402681911", "created_at": "2026-11-05T23:36:54.000Z", "edit_history_tweet_ids": ["2118487751607697688"], "entities": {"mentions": [{"end": 12, "id": "1209960539390201864", "start": 0, "username": "dwarkesh_sp"}]}, "id": "2118487751607697688", "public_metrics": {"bookmark_count": 0, "impression_count": 97, "like_count": 3, "quote_count": 1, "reply_count": 0, "retweet_count": 0}, "referenced_tweets": [{"id": "2118449808402681911", "type": "replied_to"}], "text": "@dwarkesh_sp We have precious little time. Please dwarkesh if you make evals, spend your efforts on targets for good things.\n\nThis is cool but not good. Quality evals are signposts for optimization. They will be hillclimbed by some org eventually. Ask yourself, do you want this hillclimbed?"}, {"author_id": "411869974", "conversation_id": "2118449808402681911", "created_at": "2026-11-05T23:23:51.000Z", "edit_history_tweet_ids": ["2118484468457750579"], "entities": {"mentions": [{"end": 12, "id": "1209960539390201864", "start": 0, "username": "dwarkesh_sp"}]}, "id": "2118484468457750579", "note_tweet": {"text": "I'm probably one of the best BotC players in the world. I've tried many times over the past 2 years to get LLMs to understand the game, but it has consistently failed. The games involved intricate interactions between many characters, which LLMs fail to track, at most they they reason about interactions between pairs of characters. E.g. imagine a Poppygrower game where the demon voted to kill a minion, which a Cannibal ate and got poisoned info, but Oracle got 0 because they're next to No Dashii, while the drunk Village Idiot got good on the demon and the sober Village Idiot is Cerenovus mad. This is reasonable for a human player to track, but I haven't been able to get LLMs to come close.\nLet alone understanding theory of mind, they have no idea what a real player would be thinking.\nJust lying convincingly is the least interesting part of LLMs + BotC imo. I've participated in an AI Werewolf tournament and the best bot I've made just lied about the rules of the game and said stuff like \"earlier the storyteller announced I was good, you just forgot\" to never get executed, which I think is as good as current LLMs can get.\nImo it would be far more interesting to get agents to simulate each other player's beliefs. E.g. assuming players have perfect logic, how can evil players maximize the number of valid worlds where the real demon isn't believed to be a demon. I couldn't get a pure LLM to come close, it needed a script to track worlds."}, "public_metrics": {"bookmark_count": 1, "impression_count": 77, "like_count": 2, "quote_count": 0, "reply_count": 1, "retweet_count": 0}, "referenced_tweets": [{"id": "2118449808402681911", "type": "replied_to"}], "text": "@dwarkesh_sp I'm probably one of the best BotC players in the world. I've tried many times over the past 2 years to get LLMs to understand the game, but it has consistently failed. The games involved intricate interactions between many characters, which LLMs fail to track, at most they they"}, {"author_id": "1508051347094003717", "conversation_id": "2118449808402681911", "created_at": "2026-11-05T22:40:05.000Z", 